      "problem": "$\\sqrt{64{p}^{2}}=$",
      "worked_solution": "Remember that the square root of a perfect square can be simplified using absolute value.\n\n$$\n\\sqrt{{x}^{2}}=|x|\n$$\n\nAlso, we notice that $64$ and ${p}^{2}$ are both perfect squares.\n\nFirst, we write the expression under the square root as a perfect square:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{64{p}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{{8}^{2}\\cdot {p}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8\\cdot 8\\cdot p\\cdot p} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8\\cdot p\\cdot 8\\cdot p} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{8p\\cdot 8p} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{(8p)^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we take the absolute value of the squared quantity and simplify using the rules of absolute value:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{(8p)^{2}} & =|8p| \\\\  & =|8|\\cdot |p| \\\\  & =8|p|\\end{aligned}\n$$",
